diff --git a/docs/UPGRADE.md b/docs/UPGRADE.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..e8be245
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/UPGRADE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,68 @@
+# Upgrade compatibility record
+
+## Ruby 3.2 / Rails 6.1 release
+
+- Production moves from Ruby 3.0 to Ruby 3.2 while remaining on Rails 6.1.7.10.
+- `Gemfile` is the production bundle. `BUNDLE_GEMFILE=Gemfile.next` uses the same
+  dependencies with Rails 7.0.8.7 and has its own `Gemfile.next.lock`.
+- Keep `secret_key_base` and the configured Redmine `secret_token` stable for the
+  deployment and rollback windows. Do not rotate them during the Rails upgrade.
+- `config.load_defaults` follows the booted Rails version. The Rails 6.1 bundle
+  adopts the Rails 7 SHA-256 digest and key-generator settings early. Cookie
+  serialization is temporarily `:hybrid`, so Rails 6.1 reads old Marshal
+  cookies and writes Rails 7-compatible JSON cookies. Rails 7 uses its own 7.0
+  framework defaults and is the forward writer for the next release.
+  Rails 7 still has the `:json` default: a verified legacy Marshal payload is
+  accepted only when it has Marshal's signature, then rewritten as JSON. No Rails
+  7 framework default is globally overridden.
+- Rails 6.1 has a small key-generator compatibility shim because its native
+  implementation predates the Rails 7 `hash_digest_class` option. It makes the
+  Rails 6.1 rollback build read new authenticated Rails 7 cookies and write the
+  same format. Cookie rotations continue to accept the old Rails 6.1 SHA-1 AES-CBC
+  cookie format.
+- `Mailer` mail links retain their route, purpose, expiry, and payload. Their
+  verifier uses the Rails 7 key derivation immediately and verifies pre-upgrade
+  SHA-1-derived links as a rotation. This protects mail sent before either
+  upgrade as well as mail sent by Rails 7 if it is rolled back to Rails 6.1.
+- Database-backed account, recovery, registration, API, feed, session, and
+  autologin tokens are unchanged and do not need migration.
+- Date and time fixture/query formatting uses `to_formatted_s(:db)`, which
+  works on both Rails versions without overriding Rails 7's removed argumented
+  `to_s` compatibility setting.
+- Existing nullable `belongs_to` semantics are declared with `:optional => true`
+  per association while Rails keeps `belongs_to_required_by_default` at its
+  framework default. Nested query and account-registration session hashes are
+  read through indifferent access because JSON normalizes symbol keys to strings.
+- API template format `:api` is registered for Rails 7's resolver, while the API
+  builder restores the requested JSON/XML content type. Same-host referers are
+  validated before `redirect_to` opts through Rails 7's open-redirect guard;
+  invalid or external referers still use the action's normal fallback.
+- Compatibility tests exercise legacy SHA-1/AES-CBC/Marshal cookies, Rails 7
+  SHA-256/AES-GCM/JSON cookies, and mail-link verifier rotations under both
+  bundles.
+
+## Rollout and rollback
+
+1. Deploy this Ruby 3.2 / Rails 6.1 build with the default `Gemfile` first.
+2. Keep the existing application secrets and host/protocol configuration.
+3. Once the release is healthy, the next release can switch to
+   `BUNDLE_GEMFILE=Gemfile.next` and run Rails 7.0 on its framework defaults.
+4. A rollback from that Rails 7 release must return to this Rails 6.1 build, not
+   the prior Ruby 3.0 build, so that it can read Rails 7 cookies and mail links.
+
+## Next release and cleanup
+
+- Promote Rails 7.0.8.7 from `Gemfile.next` to the production `Gemfile` only in
+  the Rails release. Continue testing the retained Rails 6.1 bundle as the
+  rollback target.
+- Keep the SHA-1 key rotations through the Rails 7 rollback window and until
+  all pre-upgrade 30-day mail links have expired. After rollback no longer
+  targets this release and active legacy cookies have migrated, remove the
+  Rails 6.1 key-generator shim, legacy cookie rotations, and mail verifier
+  rotation. Remove the authenticated Marshal JSON fallback only after the
+  same legacy-cookie window has closed.
+- Rails 7.0 deprecations for later releases include direct
+  `ActiveRecord::Base.default_timezone` use and implicit object-to-string
+  conversion in `SafeBuffer`; address those only with the Rails 7.1 work.
+- Re-enable only defaults introduced after Rails 7.0 in a later, separately
+  planned release; do not combine that work with the Rails 7 promotion.
